Members of the Welsh National Assembly are afraid that they look like Muppets
Some members of the National Assembly are stunned by the recommendation to change their title to MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
The theme has started with a plan to change the name of the National Assembly to the Welsh Parliament.
Representatives of all political tendencies in the National Assembly are concerned that this could lead to ridiculement.
A member of the Labour National Assembly said his group members were concerned because it rhymed with the words “Twp and Pwp.”
An explanation for readers outside of Wales: the Welsh word daft means poop and pwp means poop.
A member of Plaid said that the whole group as a whole “is not happy” and has come up with other names.
A member of the Welsh Conservative Party said his group was “sympathetical” about the name change, but noted that the word “MWP” is very similar to “Muppet”.
To clarify: in Welsh, the pronunciation of the letter w is similar to the pronunciation in Yorkshire.
The National Assembly Committee, which is currently drafting a bill on the name change, said: “Of course, the members themselves will make a final decision on all the titles by which members of the National Assembly are invited.”
Welsh Government Act of 2017 gave the Welsh National Assembly the right to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public poll of proposals concerning the name, which broadly supports the idea of renaming the National Assembly as the Welsh Parliament.
With regard to the official title of members of the National Assembly, the committee preferred “Members of the Wales Parliament or WMPs”, but participants in the public poll were most in favour of the “MWP” option.
Members of the Assembly are likely to propose other designations, but reaching an agreement may be an ordeal for Elin Jones, who is expected to propose amendments in the coming weeks.
The reform legislation also includes other changes in the working principles of the National Assembly, including the rules on deprivation of the rights of members of the National Assembly and the structure of the committee system.
The final vote on the appointment of members of the National Assembly will remain with the members themselves in the discussion of the legislation.
The Macedonians will hold a referendum on changing the country's name
On Sunday, voters will vote on whether to renate their country as “Northern Macedonian Republic”.
A referendum will be helded with the aim of resolving a decades-long dispute with neighboring Greece, which has its own province, Macedonia.
Athens has long claimed that its northern neighbour's name represents a claim for this territory and has repeatedly objected to running for EU and NATO membership.
Macedonian President Gjorge Ivanov, who opposes a referendum on a name change, has said that he intends to ignore the vote.
Supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zayev, argue that the name change is just a price that the country would pay for joining the EU and NATO.
St. Martin's bells are silent because Harlem's churches are struggling
“The older people I’ve talked to have said there used to be a bar and a church on every corner,” said Mr. Adams.
“There is neither.”
He said the disappearance of bars is understandable.
“People communicate differently these days,” he said.
