However, when it comes to churches, he is concerned that the proceeds from the sale of assets will not last as long as the leaders hope, and “sooner or later they will be back at the beginning.”
Adams added that churches can be replaced by apartment buildings whose apartments are full of people who do not lend a helping hand to the remaining sanctuaries in the neighborhood.
“The vast majority of people who buy apartments in these buildings are white,” he added, “who will therefore speed up that day when all these churches close their doors, as it’s unlikely that most of the people who settle here will become members of those churches.”
Both churches were built by white congregations before Harlem became a black metropoly − Metropolitan Community was built in 1870, St. Martin's Church ten years later.
The original White Methodist Church passed away in the 1930s.
The Black Congregation, who had previously worshipped a god nearby, took over the ownership of the building.
St. Martin's church was seized by an African-American congregation led by reverent John Howard Johnson, who also led a boycott against the 125th Street shopkeepers on Harlem's main shopping street when they refused to hire or promote black people.
In the 1939 fire, the building was severely damaged, but when members of Father Johnson's parish were planning to restore the building, they adopted a carillion, or bell.
Father Johnson’s son and heir in St. Martin’s church, Reverend David Johnson proudly called the carillion “the bells of the poor.”
However, the expert who played Karillion in July gave it a different name: “cultural value” and “irreplaceable historical means”.
Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan also noted that this is the world’s first carillion played by black musician Dionisio A. Lind, who continued to play the biggest carillony in Riverside Church 18 years ago.
Mr Merriweather said St. Martin's Church didn't replace him.
What has been played in St. Martin's Church in recent months has been the story of architects and contractors, some of whom have been involved by the church's secular leaders, others by the diocese.
In July, the governing body of the church, i.e. the governing body of a congregation consisting of secular leaders, complained to the bishopric in writing that the bishopric “tries to pass on costs”, although the presidency was not even involved in hiring architects and contractors sent by the diocese.
Some members of the association complained about the poor transparency of the actions of the diocese.
Shark injured 13-year-old boy diving for lobsters
Authorities said a 13-year-old boy was attacked and injured by a 13-year-old boy diving for lobsters on the opening day of the lobster season in California.
Attacking took place just before 7 a.m
Chad Hammel told San Diego's KSWB-TV that he had been diving for about half an hour with his friends on a Saturday morning when he heard the boy's cries for help and then swam over with the others to help pull the boy out of the water.
Hammel said he initially thought that the boy expressed his fascination for getting the lobster, but “then realized that the boy shouted “I was bitten!
I was bitten!”
His entire clavicle was snatched,” Hammel described the sight he saw when he got to the boy.
“I cried out to everyone to come out of the water: “There’s a shark in the water!”, Hammel added.
The life-threatening boy was taken by plane to Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego.
The species of attacked shark has so far been unknown.
Lifeguard and Captain Larry Giles said at a press conference that sharks had already been spotted in the area a few weeks earlier, but then it was determined that this was not a dangerous shark species.
Giles added that the victim's injuries were at the top of the torson.
For investigative and safety purposes, the authorities closed access to Ponto Beach in Ecinitas from Casablad to Swami for 48 hours.
Giles added that there are more than 135 species of sharks in the region, but most of them are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury’s plans to take power in the UK beauty market
Sainsbury’s takes over Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams chains, creating supermarket-like beauty aisles where expert advisors also work.
As part of a significant intrusion into the UK's 2.7-billion-pound beauty market, which continues to rise as the sales of fashion and household goods decline, the wider line-ups of beauty products are being tested in 11 stores across the country, introducing them in other stores next year if they are successful.
Investment in beauty products is because supermarkets are looking for ways to take advantage of the shelf space previously allocated for TVs, microwave ovens and household goods.
Sainsbury's said it plans to double its range of beauty products up to 3000 products, including brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw for the first time.
Existing products from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees are also given more space in brands such as those currently available in Boots stores.
Also, the supermarket is launching its make-up line Boutique again, in which the majority of products are suitable for vegans, as young shoppers demand more and more.
In addition, perfume retailer Fragrance Shop is testing sole permits in two Sainsbury’s stores, the first of which opened last week in Croydon, South London, and the second opens this year at Selly Oak, Birmingham.
Online shopping and switching to the daily purchase of small amounts of food from local grocery stores means that supermarkets need to try harder to convince people to visit them.
Sainsbury CEO Mike Coupe has said outlets are becoming more and more similar to supermarkets, as the supermarket chain tries to combat low-cost chains such as Aldi and Lidl, offering more services and manufactured goods.
Sainsbury's has put Argos outlets in hundreds of stores. The same has been done with Habitatsi outlets, as Sainsbury’s bought both chains two years ago, which have allegedly supported grocery sales and made acquisitions more profitable.
Supermarket's earlier attempt to renew its beauty and treatment departments ended in failure.
Sainsbury experimented with Boots in the early 2000s, but it ended in a quarrel about how to share the proceeds from the sale of pharmacy goods in supermarkets.
The new strategy was developed after Sainsbury’s selled his 281 sons’ pharmaceutical business three years ago for 125 million pounds to Celesio, which owns the entire Lloyds Pharmacy retail chain.
Lloyds also plays a role in the strategy by incorporating luxury skin care products from brands such as La Roche-Posay and Vichy.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Sainsbury's business manager, said: “We’ve changed the appearance and nature of our beauty products aisles in order to improve the surrounding environment for our customers.
We have also spent money on specially trained employees who are always ready for advice.
However, the attractive environment and convenient locations mean that we are now a competing beauty shopping destination, which can change old ways of shopping.”
Peter Jones is furious as Holly Willoughby withdrew the 11 million pound deal
Dragons Den’s star Peter Jones left “furiously” after TV presenter Holly Willoughby withdrew an 11-million-pound deal in his lifestyle brand business to focus on new contracts with Marks and Spencer and ITV.
Willoughby lacks time for home goods and clothing brand Truly.
Couple's business has been compared to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
The 37-year-old “This Morning” host has announced his departure on Instagram.
Holly Willoughby left Dragons Den's star, Peter Jones, angry, stepping out of their luxury lifestyle brand at the last minute to focus on their new successful contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
