Killed a Chinese peasant to death
According to local media reports, a peasant was attacked on a market in southwestern China by a pig that killed a man.
A man who was identified only by the surname Yuan was found dead with an injured artery and covered with blood on the Liupanshui market in Guizhou province, the Southern Chinese newspaper “Morning Post” reported on Sunday.
On 30 May 20, 2005 the pig keeper prepared to vaccinate pigs in his barn at his barn in Qinghai Province, Xining, China.
It is said that he had come with his cousin from the neighboring province Yunnan on Wednesday to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The next morning, his cousin found him dead and discovered that an adjacent feather had been opened.
He said there was a big, bloody-mouthed squirrel.
The forensic investigation confirmed that 249.47 kilograms of squirrels, according to the report, had killed the peasant.
“My cousin’s feet were completely bloody and crushed,” the Guiyang Evening News newspaper said.
Security camera footages showed Yuan entered the market at 04:40 a.m. on Thursday to feed his pigs.
His body was found nearly an hour later.
The animal that killed the man didn't belong to Yuan or his cousin.
The market manager told Evening News that the pig had been locked up so he wouldn't be able to attack anyone else while police were gathering evidence at the scene.
Yuani's family and market leaders are reportedly discussing compensation for the man's death.
Although rarely, it has also previously been observed that pigs attack people.
