Brett Kavanaugh’s hearing was heard by more than 20 million people
More than 20 millions of people on Thursday watched Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh and Christine Blasey Ford, a woman who allegedly accused him of sexual violence in the 1980s.
At the same time, a political impasse continued in which broadcasters interrupted the showing of conventional broadcasts to cover Friday's last-minute recess: Arizona Senator Jeff Flake drafted an agreement with the FBI to conduct a one-week investigation into the allegations.
Ford told the Senate Judiciary Commission that it was absolutely certain that Kavanaugh grabbed him drunk and tried to tear off her clothes at a high school graduation party.
Kavanaugh, in his passionate confession, said it was absolutely certain that this did not happen.
More than 20.4 million people were likely to watch the broadcast, according to Nielsen's report on Friday.
The company calculated the average audience on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
Figures weren't immediately available on other channels that showed it, including PBS, C-SPAN, and the Fox Business Network.
Nielsen also usually has trouble calculating the number of people who watch transfers in the authorities.
By way of comparison, this is about the same audience as it is at the football finals or the Oscars.
Fox News Channel opinion leaders, who have strongly supported Kavanaugh’s appointment, led all networks with an average of 5.69 million viewers during a full-day session, Nielsen said.
ABC with 3.26 million viewers came in second place.
CBS had 3.1 million, NBC had 2.94 million, MSNBC had 2.89 million viewers, and CNN had 2.52 million viewers, Nielsen said.
There was also a lot of interest after the hearing.
Flake was the central character of Friday's drama.
