Earthquake and tsunami in Indonesia have killed thousands, the death toll rises
At least 384 people were killed and many washed away when tsunamis invaded the beach after a strong earthquake and tsunami struck the island of Sulawesi in Indonesia, authorities said on Saturday.
Hundreds of people had come together on Friday for the beach festival in the city of Palu, when six-metre waves hit the beach, killing a lot of people and destroying everything that was left on the road.
The tsunami followed a 7.5 magnitude earthquake.
“When the tsunamic threat arose yesterday, people still did what they were doing on the beach, rather than fleeing immediately, thus remaining victims,” Sutopo Purwo Purwo Nugroho, a spokeswoman for the Indonesian Disasterous Alleviation Agency, told a press conference in Jakarta.
“The tsunami didn’t come alone, but took cars, logs and houses with him, sweeping everything from the ground,” Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami was moving offshore at speeds of 800 km per hour before arriving ashore.
Some of the people climbed trees to escape the tsunami and survived, he said.
Approximately 16,700 people were evacuated to Palu 24.
Aerial photographs published by the Disaster Board showed a number of destroyed buildings and shops, broken and collapsed bridges and mosques surrounded by water.
Aftermath in the coastal city continued on Saturday.
In an area with a population of 2.4 million, a wave of earthquakes was felt.
Indonesia's Office for the Evaluation and Execution of Technology (BPPT) said in a statement that the energy released on Friday after a strong earthquake was nearly 200 times higher than that of a nuclear bomb that was dropped into Hiroshima in World War II.
The geography of the city along a long and narrow bay may have increased tsunami strength, BPPT said.
Nugroho described the damage as “extensive” and said that thousands of houses, hospitals, supermarkets and hotels had collapsed.
He said the bodies of some of the victims were found under the wreckage of collapsed buildings, adding that 540 people were injured and 29 missing.
Nugroho said the death toll and damage may be higher in a coastal area 300 km north of Palus called Donggala, which was closer to the center of the earthquake.
Notifications from Donggala “are completely incomplete and do not contain any information,” Nugroho said.
“There are more than 300 000 residents living in the area,” the Red Cross said in a statement, adding that its team and volunteers are heading to affected places.
“It’s already a tragedy that could get even worse,” he said.
The agency was widely criticized on Saturday for not reporting tsunamis in Palu, although authorities said the waves reached the beach at the same time the warning was issued.
Amateur footage on social media shows a man on the top floor of a building who is panickedly warninging people on the street below for a tsunami approaching.
Minutes later, the wall of water arrives on the beach, carrying houses and cars.
Reuters could not immediately confirm the correctness of the material.
The earthquake and tsunami caused a large-scale blackout, which hindered communication in the Palu area and complicated the organization of rescue efforts by the authorities.
The military has started sending transport planes from Jakarta and other cities, authorities said, but evacuators still urgently need food and other basic supplies.
The city-based airport was reopened for rescue efforts, otherwise remaining closed until October.
President Joko Widodo plans to visit evacuation centers on Sunday.
More than 800 deaths due to the Indonesian tsunami rose.
It's very bad.
