It hasn't happened yet.
“This continues to be a provincial disaster,” said Aulia Arriani, a spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
“If the government says, ‘Okay, this is a national disaster,’ we may begin to accept international aid, but such a situation has not yet been declared.”
When Palus arrived after Friday's earthquake and tsunami, the friends and families of the missing people were still full of hope that their loved ones were those miraculous cases that ease the gloom of natural disasters.
On Saturday, a little boy was lifted out of the sewerage well.
On Sunday, rescuers released a woman trapped next to her mother's body for two days under the wreckage.
