The U.S. is expected to double funding for artificial intelligence.
“As China is becoming more and more active in artificial intelligence, the U.S. is expected to double the amount allocated to research in this field,” said Kai-Fu Lee, a donor and artificial intelligence agent who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
Comments were released after various parts of the U.S. government made statements about artificial intelligence, even though the U.S. generally lacks an official artificial intelligence strategy.
At the same time, last year, China unveiled its plan to be a player in the renewal of artificial intelligence by 2030.
“Doubling an artificial intelligence research budget would be a good start, given that other countries are so much behind the U.S. and we’re acting for another breakthrough in artificial intelligence,” Lee said.
Double funding would double the likelihood that the next major artificial intelligence achievement will be made in the United States, Lee told CNBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, the CEO of Sinnovation Ventures, which funded one of the most notable artificial intelligence companies in China Face++.
In the 1980s, he worked at Carnegie Mellon University with an artificial intelligence system that defeated America’s best Othello player. He later served on the board of Microsoft Research and the president of Google’s Chinese branch.
Lee recognised previous U.S. government technology competitions such as the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency's Robotics Challenge and asked when the next contest would take place to help identify the following dreamers.
"U.S. scientists have to work hard to get funding from the government," Lee said.
“China isn’t the one who takes over intellectuals, it’s companies,” Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have hired brilliant researchers from universities to work on artificial intelligence.
Lee said changes to immigration policies could help the United States support artificial intelligence efforts.
“I think that those who have received a doctorate in artificial intelligence should be automatically offered a green card,” he said.
The State Council of China released the next-generation artificial intelligence development plan in July 2017.
“China’s National Science Foundation distributes funding to people working in academic institutions, similar to how the National Science Foundation and other government agencies allocate funds to U.S. researchers, but the quality of academic work is lower in China,” Lee said.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense established a merged artificial intelligence center, which aims to engage partners from both industry and educational institutions. However, the White House announced the formation of an artificial intelligence selected committee.
This month, DARPA announced that it started funding the two-million-dollar AI Next initiative.
However, the National Science Foundation currently finances more than US$100 million a year for research related to artificial intelligence.
At the same time, U.S. legislation aimed at setting up the National Security Commission for Artificial Intelligence has not been changed.
Macedonians to vote in referendum on change of state name
The Macedonians voted in Sunday's referendum on whether to change the country's name to the Republic of North Macedonia. This would be a step that would also resolve a decades-long dispute with Greece, which has obstructed Macedonia's approval of applications for membership of the EU and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, claims that its northern neighbour's name poses a requirement for that territory and has banned Macedonia from joining NATO and the EU.
The governments of the two countries made an agreement in June on Macedonia's new name, but nationalist opponents claim that the name would damage the national personality of Macedonia's Slavic people's majority.
President Gjorge Ivanov said that he does not intend to take part in the referendum, and the ignorance campaign has raised doubts as to whether the turnout will reach the 50% participation rate required for the referendum.
In a referendum, they ask: “Are you in favour of Macedonia’s accession to NATO and the EU by agreeinging an agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that this is the price to be paid by Macedonia, one of the countries established since the collapse of Yugoslavia, to ensure membership with organisations such as the EU and NATO.
“Today I came here today to vote for the future of our country and the youth of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the protection of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us,” said 79-year-old Olivera Georgievska, who voted in Skopje.
Although the vote is not legally binding, a sufficient number of Members have said that they will comply with the outcome of the vote in order to make it decisive.
A two-thirds majority in Parliament is necessary for a name change.
The National Electoral Committee said there were no reports of irregularities at 13:00.
However, the turnout was only 16 percent, compared to 34 percent in the previous 2016 parliamentary elections, when 66 percent of registered voters voted.
“I came to vote for my children. Our place is in Europe,” said 62-year-old Gjose Tanevsky, who voted in Skopje, the capital of Macedonia.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and their son Dushko, gave Strumica their vote on 30 September 2018 in a Macedonian referendum on changing the country's name, which would give them the opportunity to join NATO and the European Union.
In front of the parliament building in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov prepared a small stage, lifting chairs in front of the tents for those who ignore the referendum.
“We want to belong to NATO and the EU, but we want to join our heads up, not through the servants’ door,” Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we still have dignity.
If they don’t want to accept us as Macedonia, we can appeal to others, such as China and Russia, and get from European-Asian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev said that membership of NATO would bring the necessary investments in Macedonia at an unemployment rate of more than 20 percent.
“I believe that a large majority will support changing the name, because more than 80 percent of citizens are in favor of membership of the EU and NATO,” Zaev said.
He said that the “yes-word” would be “confirmation of our future.”
According to a poll published Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Political Studies, 30 to 43 percent of voters intend to take part in the referendum, which is below the required turnout.
A poll conducted by Telma TV in Macedonia has revealed that 57% of respondents plan to vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70 percent said they would say “yes.”
The success rate of the referendum is 50 percent plus one vote.
The failure of the referendum would represent the first serious blow to the government's Western politics since the government took over last May.
