The Labour Party is no longer a great association.
It is intolerable to those who express their opinion
When the activists of my local Momentum voted to condemn me, it wasn't very surprising.
After all, I am the last one on the Labour List who is told that you are not welcome, all for expressing your opinion.
My colleague from Parliament, Joan Ryan, received the same treatment because she stood strongly for anti-Jewishness.
In my case, I was judged by those who condemned me for disagreeing with Jeremy Corbyn.
Responsible economic policies, national security and European issues, which are bizarrely similar issues that Jeremy disagreed on with previous leaders.
Nottingham East’s Labour Party tweeted that “we want meetings to be inclusive and creative.”
For the most part, my eight-year-old Local Labour Member's experience with Friday Night's General Assembly meetings is exactly like this.
As of today, unfortunately, this no longer applies to most meetings, and the promise of being “kinder and more generous” in politics has long been forgotten, if it was ever remembered.
It is becoming increasingly apparent that different perspectives are not acceptable in the Leiborist Party, and for each opinion it is judged who is acceptable to the party's leadership.
It started shortly after Jeremy became a leader. The co-worker, with whom I previously thought to share similar political views, was now waiting for me to change my mind and accept positions that I would otherwise not have agreed to, regardless of whether it was issues of national security or the EU's single market.
Wheneverthily speaking somewhere, no matter what I'm actually talking about, it'll be condemned on social media, prompting my recall, ignoring the party's policies and saying I shouldn't belong to the Leiborist party.
It’s not just my personal experience.
Indeed, I know that I am more lucky than some of my collaborators, since the comments directed at me are mostly political.
I admire the competence and decision-makingness of those co-workers who face sexist or racist abuse every day, but never give up.
One of the most disappointing positions in this policy area is how levels of abuse have become commonplace.
Jeremy Corbyn said last week that the Labour Party should promote a culture of tolerance.
However, the reality is that we are no longer such a large association, and with every “untrustrity” initiative or change of election rules, the party becomes narrower.
In the last two years, I’ve been given a lot of advice to keep my head down, talk less, and “then everything would be fine.”
But that’s not what I came into politics.
From the moment I joined the Leiborist Party 32 years ago as a schoolchild, motivated by the negligence of Thatcher's government, which had left my large school classroom literally on the verge of collapse, I have tried to speak for better public services for those who need it most, acting both as a member of a local council member and a government minister.
I've never concealed my politics, including in recent elections.
Under no circumstances is it possible that anyone in Nottingham East was confused about my political positions or areas of disagreement with local leadership.
What I would like to say is that if the country is moving towards a Brexit that harms households, businesses and our public services, I do not understand the desire to spend my time and energy, being loyal to the leader of the Labour Party.
But, in fact, the only message I have is not intended for the Nottingham Momentum, but for my supporters, regardless of whether they belong to the Labour Party or not: I am proud to serve you, and I promise that not one removal threat or political consideration will stop me from acting for what I think is in the best interests of all of you.
Chris Leslie is a Member of the Nottingham East
Ayr 38–17 Melrose: Invincible Ayr moves to the top
