“The sea is us”: Bolivia without maritime borders hopes the court will reopen their way to the Pacific
Sailors are patrolling the naval headquarters in La Paz.
Ocean-blue flags are flying above the authority's buildings.
A slogan has been painted for naval bases from Lake Titicaca to Amazon: “The sea is our right.
It’s our responsibility to restore it.”
Completely without maritime borders in Bolivia is the memory of the coastal line, which was lost to Chile in a bloody resource conflict in the 19th century, still alive. Equally lively is the desire to sail again in the Pacific Ocean.
These hopes are perhaps the highest in recent decades, as Bolivia is awaiting a ruling by an international court on 1 October after five years of negotiations.
“Bolivia has momentum, a spirit of unity and tranquility, and the state, of course, waits for an abilite result,” said Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla.
A number of Bolivians from all over the country are looking at the decision of an international court on large screens, in the hope that the Hague Court will decide in favour of Bolivia, after which Chile, after decades of intermittent discussions, is obliged to negotiate with Bolivia in order to give the latter an independent path to the sea.
Bolivia's charismatic native president Evo Morales, who will face a controversial fight for re-election next year, is at stake with Monday's verdict.
“We are very close to returning to the Pacific,” he swore at the end of August.
However, some analysts believe that the court is unlikely to vote in favor of Bolivia, or if so, very little would change.
The U.N. authority in the Netherlands does not have the power to surrender Chile's territory and stipulates that it does not determine the outcome of possible negotiations.
Just six months after hearing the final statement, the international court's decision suggests it wasn’t a “complicated case,” said Paz Zárate, Chile’s expert on international law.
And moving away from the further causes of Bolivia, the past four years may have worsened the situation.
“The problem of access to the sea has been robbed by the current Bolivian authorities,” Zárate said.
Morales' belligerential eloquence has lost all remaining Chilean benevolence, he noted.
Bolivia and Chile will continue negotiations at some point, but after all this, the debate will be extremely difficult.
The two countries have not exchanged ambassadors since 19662.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, who is Bolivia's Representative in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision was unnecessarily quick.
Monday will give Bolivia an “extreme opportunity to start a new era in the country’s relations with Chile” and an opportunity to “end 139 years of discordances with mutual benefit,” he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales, who is still one of the best-known presidents in Latin America, is using the issue of the sea to express political resentment.
“Bolivia will never give up its right of access to the Pacific,” he added.
“There’s an opportunity to see that we have to overcome the past.”
North Korea said there would be no release of nuclear weapons as long as the country cannot trust the United States
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho has said his people will never give up nuclear weapons first if they can't trust Washington.
Ri spoke at the U.N. meeting on Saturday.
He urged the United States of America to review the pledges made by rival leaders during the Singapore summit.
He comments at a time when U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo almost appears to be restarting nuclear diplomacy more than three months after meeting with North Korean leader Kim Jong Uni in Singapore.
According to Ri, these are air castles, suggesting that the U.S. will continue to impose restrictions and a statement to end the war with Korea will lead North Korea to surrender.
Washington is cautious about such a statement if Pyongyang does not take the first important steps towards disarmament.
Both Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second summit.
But what is common is skepticism that Pyongyang seriously intends to give up armaments, which the state probably sees as the only way to ensure its own safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to agree on a second summit between Kim and Trump.
Paris fashion shows bring to the viewer a series of massive headgears that are delivered to shoppers on the shopping streets in their neighborhood
If you want to enlarge your hat collection or protect yourself completely from the sun, you don't have to search anymore.
Designers Valentino and Thom Browne brought a series of bizarre huge headgearts to the stages as part of their 2019 summer season, dazzling Paris Fashion Week.
Tall and impractical hats are fashionable via Instagram this summer, and these designers have sent their stunning creations to fashion stages.
Valentino's top work was an overed beige hat covering the heads of models, decorated with a wide border reminiscent of feathers.
Other super-large items of clothing included watermelons decorated with jewels, a wizard's hat and even pineapple, but their purpose is not to insulate the head.
Thom Browne also showed a strange selection of masks, doing it at exactly the right time before Halloween.
A large part of the colorful masks had sewn mouths, so they were more reminiscent of Hannibal Lecter than high fashion.
One part of the creation resembled a diving costume with an air tube and glasses, while the other resembled a melted ice cream tube.
And if you continue with the giant fads, you'll be lucky.
Fashion gurus predict that ultra-large headgears can also reach the shopping streets near you.
The ultra-large hats come right on the heels of La Bomba, a 60-centimeter-wide-brimmed hat that's been seen on all celebrities' heads from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult brand, which created a largely impractical hat on social media, sent another big work to the fashion stages – a straw beach bag that is almost as big as a bikini model carrying it.
The dark orange raffia bag with raffa fringes and a white leather handle was the top work of Jacquemus La Riviera's 2019 summer collection at Paris Fashion Week.
Celebrity stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “I look forward to seeing big hats and beach bags on shopping streets next summer. Since the designer has had such a big impact, it would be difficult to ignore the demand for oversized items of clothing.”
John Edward: Language skills are important for citizens of the world
Scottish private schools have remained at the top of the academic excellence rankings and this continued in 2018 with the following outstanding exam results, reinforced by personal and joint success in sports, arts, music, and other community ventures.
These schools, with more than 30 000 pupils across Scotland, represented by the Scottish Council of Private Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the bestest service to their students and their parents.
Private schools aim to prepare pupils for additional and higher education, their chosen profession, and their place as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that is able to design and implement a curriculum adapted to circumstances, we can see that modern languages continue to be well-known and desired subjects in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you speak to a person in a language he understands, it comes to his mind.
If you speak to him in his mother tongue, it will reach his heart.”
This is a powerful reminder that we cannot rely solely on English if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
Of this year's latest exam results, we can see that there are languages at the front ends of the tables, for which the rate of passing exams among private schools is the highest.
In total, 68 percent of the students who studied foreign languages received a high result of A.
Data collected from a total of 74 SCIS schools shows that 72 percent of students scored in Mandarin, 72 percent of German learners received the same grades, 69 percent of French language learners and 63 percent of Spanish learners.
This shows that Scottish private schools support foreign languages as life skills that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages that are now among the choices of subjects are in the curricula of private schools and other schools in the same way as STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics).
A study conducted by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills in 2014 revealed that 17% of respondents cited a lack of language skills because of the reasons why it is difficult for employers to find workers for vacancies.
Therefore, language skills are becoming increasingly mandatory in order to prepare young people for their future work.
As more predictable job offers require language proficiencies, such skills are becoming essential in a globalising world.
Regardless of the career choice, acquiring a second language gives a person a real advantage in the future, as language skills are lifelong.
The ability to communicate directly with foreign citizens immediately gives a multilingual person a competitive advantage.
According to a YouGov survey of more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 percent of respondents do not speak any foreign languages well enough to have a conversation about it. The only language with a two-seater percentage was French with 15 percent.
Therefore, the funding of language learning is currently important for today's children.
Proficiency in several languages, especially the languages of emerging economies, gives children better opportunities to find a good job.
In Scotland, different languages are taught in each school.
Hulk schools focus more on classical modern languages, while other schools teach languages that are considered to be the most important in the UK for 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Regardless of the interests of the child, private schools always have the opportunity to choose between several languages, which are taught by experts in their field.
Scottish private schools are committed to providing a learning environment that prepares and equips children with the skills they need to succeed, regardless of what the future holds for them.
It can no longer be denied that, in a globalised business environment, languages are vital for the future of the country, which is why it must also be reflected in education.
It is modern languages that should really be regarded as “international communication skills”.
Private schools continue to offer choice, diversity and exquisiteness to young Scots.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Board of Private Schools in Scotland.
