The U.S. offered more resistance in doubles, winning two games, but they couldn't make up the deficit.
Jim Furyk's side needs eight points from Sunday's 12 singles to maintain the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European beginner to win four points in a row. Together with Molinar, who is called Molliwood after this weekend, they are the second pair to have won four points in their fourth opening game in Ryder Cup history.
Having destroyed Woods and Patrick Reed in a four-poll, they continued in the same spirit, defeating Woods and America's beginner Bryson Dechambeau even more firmly: 5-4.
Woods, who dragged himself through two games on Saturday, showed a random glitter, but has now lost 19 out of 29 of his four-points and 19 doubles, resulting in seven losses in a row.
Justin Rose, who had rested up for a four-pound in the morning morning, returned with his partner Henrik Stenson in doubles, defeating 2-1 Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, who is first and third in the world rankings.
Europe did not succeed, although the competition was held in pleasant and windy weather south of Paris.
