Only twice in the history of the Ryder Cup, the team has come back from a four-point shortcoming, going into individual games, although as owners, Furyk's side only needs a tie to maintain the trophy.
Although being the second best for two days, after Sunday's counterattack, it seems that they will not succeed.
North Korea says “by no means” they are disarming themselves unilaterally due to a lack of trust
North Korea's foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday that continued restrictions exacerbated their distrust against the United States and that the country would not unilaterally abandon its nuclear weapons unilaterally under such conditions.
Ri Yong Ho said at the annual meeting of the World Organization that North Korea has taken “remarkable goodwill measures” over the past year, such as suspending nuclear and missile tests, dismantling a nuclear test site, and have pledged not to disseminate nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“But we don’t see that the U.S. responded,” he said.
“Without trusting the United States, our country lacks security, and under such circumstances, we are in no way willing to disarm ourselves unilaterally first.”
While Ri reiterated North Korea’s acquaintanceant complaints about Washington’s resistance to a “phasing-out” approach to denuclearisation, on the basis of which North Korea would be rewarded if he were taking gradual steps, it seemed important in his statement that he did not reject the unilateral abandonment of nuclear weapons, as Pjongjang has done in the past.
Ri referred to Kim Jong Uni's and Donald Trump's joint statement at a summit between the U.S. president and North Korea's leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim vowed to move towards "renuncing the Korean peninsula" and Trump pledged security guarantees to North Korea.
North Korea has wanted to formally end the 1950-53 Korean war, but the United States has said that Pyongyang must give up its nuclear weapons first.
Washington has also resisted calls to relax strict international restrictions on North Korea.
“The U.S. is calling for ‘renunciation of nuclear weapons first’ and increasing the pressure of restrictions in order to forcibly achieve their goal, even in response to a statement to the end of the war,” Ri said.
“The notion that restrictions bring us to our knees is a dream for people who don’t know us at all.
But the problem is that the continued restrictions are exacerbating our distrust.”
Ri didn't mention Kim and Trump's plans for the second summit, which the U.S. leader stressed at the U.N. earlier this week.
Instead, the minister highlighted a meeting between three Kim and South Korean leaders, Moon Jae-in, over the past five months, adding: “If South Korea, not the United States, were one of the sides of the issue of nuclear denuclearisation, the issue of nuclear weapons in the Korean Peninsula would not have reached such an impasse.”
Nevertheless, Ri’s remarks differed sharply from last year’s when he told the U.N. General Assembly that addressing the U.S. continent with North Korean missiles was inevitable after “Mr. Bad President” Trump called Kim a “missile man” on a suicide mission.
This year, Trump at the United Nations who threatened to completely destroy North Kordo last year praised Kimi to heaven for his courage to take steps towards disarmament, but added that there is still much to be done and that the restrictions must remain in place until North Korea's denuclearization.
On Wednesday, Trump said he lacked a schedule, “Whether it takes two years, three years, or five months, that doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia believe that the United Nations Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompoe told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: “Enforcing Security Council restrictions must continue vigorously and without fail until we can fully and definitively confirm the abandonment of nuclear weapons.”
The Security Council has unanimously increased the restrictions applicable to North Korea since 2006 in an attempt to prevent funding for Pyongyang's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo met on the sidelines of the U.N. General Assembly and later said he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for a second summit.
Pompeo has already visited North Korea three times this year, although the last trip did not go very well.
After meeting Pyongyang in July, he said progress had been made, after condemning North Korea in just a few hours for “gangster-like demands”.
North Korea pledged to dismantle missiles and a nuclear complex this month if the United States implemented “corresponding measures”.
He said Kim had told him that by “corresponding measures,” he meant the security guarantees pledged by Trump in Singapore and moving towards normalizing relations with Washington.
Harvard students learn how to get enough rest
This year, Harvard University is offering a new course where students have to sleep harder to fight the growing aggressive culture, where they study caffeine-rich with night-to-night learning marathons.
Pupils who love the academia of the best university in the world are often helpless when it comes to the basics of caring for themselves.
According to Professor Charles Czeisler, a sleep doctor at the Harvard School of Medicine and Brigham, an expert at the Harvard Medical School, and Professor Czeisler of the Women's Hospital, it is the first of its kind in the United States.
He was inspired by the creation of the course after he gave a speech on the topic of how sleep disorders affect learning.
“At the end, a girl came to me and asked, “Why only now are they telling me about it in my last school year?”
“He said that no one had ever told him the importance of sleep – it knocked me down,” he told Telegraph.
The course, which entered the curriculum for the first time this year, will explain to the students how good sleeping habits contribute to academic and athletic results, and also help to improve their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatrics at the Harvard School of Medicine and director of health at the university, said that the university decided to take a course of study after it was found that students were severely sleep-deprived during the work week.
The hour-long course covers a number of interactional assignments.
In one part, students can click on coffee cups, curtains, sports shoes and books, and they are explained by the effects of caffeine and light, how lack of sleep affects athletic results and the importance of bedtime.
The second part explains to the participants how long-term lack of sleep can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
The campus map, together with opposing symbols, encourages participants to think about their daily established practice.
“We don’t know that changes students’ behavior right away.
However, we believe they have the right to know – just as you have the right to know about the health effects of smoking cigarettes,” Professor Czeisler added.
According to him, nocturnal learning marathons are still proud, adding that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students rather means that lack of sleep is still a growing problem.
“Ensuring adequate high-quality bedtime should be the student’s “secret weapon” to combat stress, exhaustion, and anxiety,” he added. “It even helps against weight gain, as lack of sleep puts the brain into hunger mode, making a person constantly hungry.”
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student of chemical and physical biology from California, helped Professor Czeisler create a course, having participated in one of his classes in one of his first Harvard years.
Raymond said the course opened his eyes and inspired him to contribute to the creation of a university-wide course.
As a next step, he wants all postgraduate students to go through a similar program of study before joining a competing institution.
Professor Czeisler advised students to set an alarm clock for both bedtime and wake-up, and to be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light” generated by electronic displays and LED lighting, as this can damage their circadian rhythm and cause problems with falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga's goal defeated Gerrard's men
