Rangers substitute Glenn Middleton once again asked for a penalty kick when he went up with Jacobs, which Thomson didn't satisfy again.
Almanac: Inventor of the Geiger Counter
And now a page from our Sunday Morning Almanac: September 30, 1882, today 136 years ago and COUNTING ... the day when the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity. It was an invention that eventually led to a device known as the Geiger counter.
From this foundation of science, the Geiger counter also became the mainstay of pop culture, as in the 1950 film “Coronado Bells”, starringing these seemingly improbable cowboy scientists Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: “What’s that?”
Rogers: “It’s a Geiger counter used to find radioactive minerals such as uranium.
It is possible to hear the buckle of atoms emitted from the radioactivity of minerals when wearing these headphones.”
Evans: “Don’t tell me, it’ll simmer now!”
“Hans” Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention bearing his name lives on.
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” fraudulent cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” scammer cells and kill them
Vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize scammer cells as part of treatment
The method involves taking immune cells from the patient and modifying their cells in the laboratory
They are then able to “see” a common protein for many cancers, and they are injected back
The study vaccine shows promising results in patients with various cancers.
One woman who was being treated for the immune system with a swindler-cell-to-detection vaccine lost her ovarian cancer for more than 18 months.
The method involves the removal of immune cells from the patient. These cells are transformed in the laboratory so that they can “see” the protein that has spread to various HER2 cancers, and then these cells are injected back.
Professor Jay Berzofsky from the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland: “Our results show that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 “leads the growth of many types of cancer,” including breast, ovarian, pulmonary and colorectal cancers, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar approach, where immune cells are taken from patients and are taught to target cancer cells, has been used in the treatment of certain types of leukaemia.
Kanye West began with Trump’s supportive tribe, wearing a MAGA hat on SNL.
It didn't go very well
On the Saturday Night Live studio, Kanye heard a disparaging whistle choir towards West, following his messed-up speech in which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump and announced his candidacy for the same position in 2020.
After that night for his third song, called Ghost Town, where he wore a hat with the inscription Make America Great, he began screaming against Democrats and reiterated his support for Trump.
“I’ve talked so much to white people who ask me, ‘How can you like Trump, she’s a racist?’
“If I was so worried about racism, I’d have moved away from America a long time ago,” he said.
SNL began a device with Matt Damon, in which a Hollywood celebrity made a joke about Brett Kavanaugh's testimony in front of the Senate Judges Committee in connection with Christine Blasey Ford's alleged sexual violence.
While this episode didn't go on the air, West noising footage was uploaded to comedian Chris Rock on social media.
It remains unclear whether Rock tried to mock West's post.
West also complained to the audience that it was quite difficult for him behind the scenes in relation to his hat.
“They teased me backstage.
They said I wouldn't go there with a hat on.
They teased me!
And then they'll say that I've drowned in the place," he said according to Washington Examiner.
West continued: “You want to see a sunken place? I’m going to put on my Superman cape because it means you can’t tell me what I have to do. Do you want the world to move forward?
Try with love.”
His comments triggered viewers’ outrage at least twice, and it seemed that the SNL team was embarrassed. According to Variety, one person revealed: “The whole studio remained deadly silent.”
West had been brought to the show at the last minute to replace Ariana Grande, because the singer's ex-boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, had died a few days earlier.
West amazed many with the song “I Love it”, during which he looked like Perrier’s bottle.
West received support from Candace Turner, the leader of the conservative group TPUSA, who tweeted: “For a brave soul: THANK YOU FOR DARING TO RESIST THE COMMUNITY MASS.”
But host Karen Hunter tweeted that West was just “what he is, and it’s absolutely wonderful.”
“But I decided NOT to reward (buying my music or clothing or supporting his ‘art’), someone who, in my opinion, believes and spreads a worldview harmful to my community.
She's free.
Just like us,” he added.
The rapper tweeted that he had changed his name, saying he was now “former Kanye West.”
She's not the first singer to change her performer's name, following Diddy, who is also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
Snoop Dogg has also been called Snoop Lion and, of course, the newly departed music legend Prince, who changed his name into a symbol.
Belfast restaurant stabbing attempted murder
A 45-year-old man was stabbed after another man was stabbed at a restaurant in eastern Belfast on Friday.
According to the police, the incident took place in Ballyhackamore.
The defendant is expected to appear before Belfast magistrate court on Monday.
The prosecution will be reviewed by the public prosecutor's office.
Throne game celebrity Kit Harington does not approve of fancy masculinity
Kit Harington is best known for his sword-flashing role as Jon Snow in HBO's violent medieval fantasy series Game of Thrones.
However, the actor (31) disapproves the stereotype of an aggressive hero, saying that such roles on the screen mean that young guys often feel that they have to be tough.
Speaking to The Sunday Times Culture, Kit thought “something had gone wrong” and raised the question of how to solve the problem of masculinity in the #MeToo era.
Kit, who recently married his Game of Thrones fellow celebrity Rose Leslie (31), admitted that he feels “quite strongly” that this problem needs attention.
“At the moment, I personally feel quite strongly that we’ve gone wrong with masculinity somewhere,” he said.
“What have we taught men when they grow up, about the problem we see now?”
Kit believes that television is partly to blame for the increase in masculinity, as ultra-masculine characters are shown there.
He continued: “What is innate and what is taught?
What is taught on TV and on the streets gives young boys the feeling that they have to be just like that when they want to be men.
I think it's really one of the biggest questions of our time - how to change it?
Because obviously something has gone wrong for the young men.”
In the poll, he also admitted that, after the end of the series next summer, he will never take part in any Game of Thrones sequels, saying that he is “on the same side with his battlefields and horses.”
Starting in November, Kit is taking part in the revival of Sam Shepard's True West, which tells the story of the filmmaker and his robber brother.
The actor recently revealed that he considers meeting his wife Rose the best thing related to Game of Thrones.
“I met my wife in this series, so it gave me my future family and my future life,” he said.
Rose played Ygritte's, Kit's Character Jon Snow's Loved One, in an award-winning fantasy series with Emmy.
The couple got married in June 2018 at Leslie's family home in Scotland.
HIV/Aids: China announces 14% increase in new cases
China has reported that the number of citizens living with HIV and AIDS has skyrocketed to 14%.
According to healthcare professionals, more than 820 000 people suffer from this disease.
About 40 000 new incidents were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
Most new cases have arisen from sexingation, thus changing the past.
HIV perpetually spread through infected blood transfusions in some parts of China .
However, the number of people infected with HIV in this way had narrowed to almost zero, Chinese health officials confirmed at a conference in Yunnan province.
From year to year, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China has increased by 100,000 people.
The spread of HIV through sex is a serious problem for China's LGBT community.
Homosexuality was legalized in China in 1997, but there is arguably a large difference between LGBT people.
Due to the country's old-fashioned values, studies show that 70-90% of men who have sex with men eventually marry women.
Many transmissions of diseases are associated with insufficient sexual protection of such relationships.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to HIV medicines to solve the problem.
Maxine Waters denies leaking GOP senator data, saying they are “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories”
U.S. spokeswoman Maxine Waters on Saturday condemned allegations that an employee had posted personal information from three Republican U.S. senators on lawmakers' Wikipedia pages.
The Los Angeles Democrat confirmed that such allegations are disseminated by “ultra-right” trolls and their websites.
“Lies, lies, and even more despicable lies,” Waters said on Twitter.
Published information allegedly included home addresses and telephone numbers of U.S. senators Lindsey Graham (South Carolina) and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch (both from Utah).
The information appeared online on Thursday. It was posted by an unfamiliar person during a Senate panel hearing on Capitol Hill regarding allegations of sexual misconduct against Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
Information was leaked shortly after three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
Old-minded websites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState reported that an IP address that identifies the source of the posting was linked to the Waters office and issued membership information from an employee belonging to Waters, Hill reported.
“This unfounded statement is complete and an absolute lie,” Waters continued.
“My employees, whose personality, personal data and safety are compromised due to their fraudulent and false claims, are in no way responsible for this communication.
This unjustified statement is a complete and irrefutable lie.”
Waters' statement was quickly reviewed on the Internet, including former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
“This denial comes from anger,” Fleischer wrote.
“This suggests that he has a sense that is not appropriate for a member of Congress.
If someone's being blamed for something they haven't done, they can't get angry.
They can't be defiant.
They must not question the prosecution's motivations.
They must remain calm.”
Fleischer appeared to compare Waters' reaction to the Democrats' criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who was accused by critics of being too angry during Thursday's hearing.
Republican nominee Omar Navarro, who is running for the position of Waters in the intervening elections, also expressed his thoughts on Twitter.
“It’s important when it’s true,” he tweeted.
In a statement, Waters said his office had “warned “relevant authorities and law enforcement agencies regarding their fraudulent allegations.”
“Let’s do everything we can to identify the criminals,” he continued, “and that they should be legally held accountable for all their actions that are destructive and dangerous to all my employees.”
Johnny English Strikes Again Review - An Underexplored Rowan Atkinson Spy Parody-Parody
It is now customary to look for the meanings of Brexit in all new British films, and this seems to be particularly appropriate in the parody series of Johnny English's action comedy, which began in 2003 with Johnny English and was sequeled in the revival of Johnny English Reborn in 2011.
Is it humorous to joking ourselves about how obviously stupid we are in the country's new export opportunity?
In any case, the incompetent Johnny English with a rubber-faced bulge has received permission to do things once again in its mischievous way – and that this broad comical creation is designed for non-English-speaking film territories.
Of course, it’s an undercover agent who, despite its bizarre pretentious smoothie glamour, is a little reminiscent of Clouseau, a bit of Mr. Bean, and a little bit of a character who set the tone for Chariots of Fire at the opening ceremony of the London 2012 Olympics.
It is also based on the character traveler and international mystery Atkinson, who was once featured in forgotten Barclaycard's TV commercials, who leaves only chaos.
There are only one or two pleasant moments in this last JE movie.
I liked the approach to the Johnny English helicopter when he wore a medieval armor and the helicopter impellers for a moment against his helmet.
Atkinson’s talent of doing physical comedy is shown, but humour seems less important and weirdly superfluous, especially when “serious” spy movies such as 007 and Mission Impossible also provide self-confident comedy.
It seems this humour is more intended for children than adults, and for me, Johnny English's cunning adventures aren't as resourceful and focused as Atkinson's silent films as Bean.
There is forever a presumption that Britain is in serious trouble.
A cyber hacker has invaded Britain's top-secret spies' online network, exposing the personalities of all of Britain's secret police officers, thereby disappointinging an on-site agent - an unfortunately small role for Kevin Eldon.
It's the last straw for the Prime Minister who's solemn and troubled character who suffers from the burden of political unpopularity: Emma Thompson does this Teresa-May's character perfectly, but there'sn't very much to work on in the manuscript.
His intelligence advisers suggest that since all active spies are endangered, he will have to recall someone who is retired.
And that means Johnny English, who now works as a schoolmaster in a fancy institution, giving secret lessons on how to be a secret agent: here are a couple of good places where English offers a School of Rock spy school.
English will be flown back to Whitehall for extraordinary information and reunited with his former long-time partner, Bough, played by Ben Miller again.
Bough is now married to a submarine captain, where actress Vicki Pepperdine’s talents have been severely wasted.
So Batman and Robin, the embarrassments of her Majesty's Secret Service are once again in action, in places Olga Kurylenko's embodied beautiful femme fatale Ophelia Bulletova.
At the same time, the Prime Minister drops dangerously into a charismatic technical-billionaire, claiming to be able to solve all British computer problems: the unfortunate Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough embark on their stubborn odyssey: by hiding as waitresses, they set fire to a French restaurant; they create chaos, secretly hiding Volta's luxury yacht aboard; and English launches a genuine mess as he tries to use a virtual reality headset to get acquainted with the interior of Volta's house.
There are no brakes used in this last section, but as cute and impetuous as it is, the whole thing has a children's TV taste.
Quite mediocre stuff.
And, as with other Johnny English films, I caught myself thinking: does the British film industry really not have a role for Rowan Atkinson that would be fair to his talent?
The Labour Party denies drawing up a plan in which the British work four days a week, but they are paid for five days
Jeremy Corbyn's Labour Party has considered a pivotal plan that requires the British to work four days a week, but they are paid for five days.
The party wants the company's superiors to communicate the savings received through the Artificial Intelligence Revolution (AI), giving them one extra day off.
That would be a three-day weekend for the employees - but they would receive the same salary.
According to the source, this idea would fit with the party's economic agenda, and so the state is going to tilt in favour of
Thursday's working week has been approved by the Congress of the Union of Trades, so that workers can take advantage of the changing economy.
A labour party source told The Sunday Times: "It is expected that the revision of the bill will be announced before the end of the year.
“It’s not happening overnight, but Thursday’s working week is an aspiration that is compatible with the party’s approach to balancing the economy, both for the benefit of the workers and with the party’s overall industrial strategy.
The Labour Party is not the first to support such an idea, as the Green Party also promised a four-day working week during its 2017 election campaign.
At the moment, however, the Labour Party as a whole does not support the aspiration.
A Labour Party spokesperson said: “Thursday Labor Week is not a party’s policy and the party doesn’t discuss it.”
Undersecretary John McDonnell used last week's Labor Party Conference to clarify his vision of a socialist turmoil in the economy.
McDonnell said he had decided to withdraw power from “faceless executives” and “profit-makers” of utilities.
The vice-chancellor’s plans also mean that the current shareholders of the water companies may not regain all their shareholding, because the Labour Party could make “deductions” in relation to the perceived misdemeanour.
He reaffirmed his plan to put employees on the company's board of directors and create inclusive ownership funds to provide 10% of the equity of private companies to employees, instead of giving annual bonuses of up to 561.65 euros.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy on “60 Minutes,” whether the FBI’s Kavanaugh investigation could change their minds
Investigating the FBI's charges against Judge Brett Kavanaugh has delayed the final voting for the Supreme Court for at least one week and raises the question of whether the bureau's findings could have an impact on ending support for Republican senators.
In Sunday’s poll, Scott Pelley’s Republican senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham, co-hosted at 60 Minutes, asked the FBI to reveal something that could make them change their minds.
Kennedy seemed more open than his colleague from South Carolina.
"Of course," Kennedy said.
“I said I was going for an interrogation. I said I had spoken to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after the charges came out and said, “Did you do that?”
He was decisive, firm and unambiguous.”
Graham's voice seems rock solid.
“I am confident in my decision regarding Brett Kavanaugh and it could only be changed by a bombshell,” he said.
“Dr. Ford, I don’t know how this happened, but I know Brett strongly denied it,” Graham added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
“And not everyone he has mentioned could confirm it.
It's a 36-year-old thing.
“I don’t see anything new coming.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival and has it contributed to reducing poverty?
This Saturday will host an annual music event at the New York Global Citizen Festival, which has hugely impressive celebrity attendance and an equally impressive goal: to eradicate poverty from the world.
The Global Citizen Festival, held for the seventh time, now sees tens of thousands of people in Central Park's Great Lawn area not only enjoying singers such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also raising awareness about the true goal of the event, which is ending extreme poverty by 2030.
Launched in 2012, the Global Citizen Festival is an extension of the Global Poverty Project, an international group of lawyers who hope to end poverty by constantly increasing the number of people struggling against it.
To get a free ticket for the event (unless you want to pay for a VIP ticket), concertgoers had to perform a number of tasks or voluntary activities, send an e-mail to some of the world's leaders, call or do anything else important to help raise awareness of their goal in relation to the eradication of poverty.
But how successful has Global Citizen been if it is still 12 years to achieve their goal?
Is the idea of giving people a free concert a real way to convince them to take action, or is it just another click where people feel that they are doing something important by signing an application online or tweeting?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has registered more than 19 million “activities” from its supporters in an attempt to achieve a number of different goals.
According to it, these measures have helped world leaders report more than US$37 billion into commitments and policies that will affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group cited 390 commitments and communications arising from their activities, of which at least US$10 billion has already been disbursed or funded.
The group estimates that nearly 649 million people around the world have so far been directly affected by guaranteed funds.
Some of the key commitments include The Power of Nutrition, a UK-based partnership for investors and implementers committed to “help taking advantage of all of their children’s abilities, promising to give Rwanda US$35 million to help end malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4700 tweets from Global Citizens.
“With the support of the UK government, donors, national governments and global citizens such as you, we can turn malnutrition into a historic footnote to social injustice,” Tracey Ullman, an ambassador at The Power of Nutrition, told the crowd during a London concert in April 2018.
The group also reported that after more than 5,000 activities calling in the United Kingdom to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government announced the funding project The Power of Nutrition, which will reach 5 million women and children in need of nutritional intervention.
In response to one FAQ on its website asking, “what makes you think we can end extreme poverty?”
Global Citizen replied, “It’s a long and hard road – sometimes we fall and fail.
However, just as the great civil rights and anti-apartheid movements we face, we are confident in our success, because together we are more powerful.
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are just a couple of performers performing at this year's event in New York hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
The U.S. could use a naval “barrier” to hinder Russia’s energy exports — interior minister
According to the Washington Examiner, Washington can use its navy, if necessary, to avoid capturing the Russian energy market, including in the Middle East, U.S. Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke has announced.
Zinke argued that Russia's participation in Syria, especially if it operates at the invitation of a legitimate government, is a pretext for exploring new energy markets.
“I believe that the reason they are in the Middle East is because they want to mediate energy, as they do in Eastern Europe, in southern Europe,” he said.
And according to the official, there are ways and means to solve it.
“The United States will have this opportunity, with the help of our navy, to ensure that naval lines are open and blocked if necessary to ensure that their energy does not enter the market,” he added.
Zinke’s statement was appealed to participants of an event hosted by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit organization that represents the “energy-consumer voice” of the United States.
He went to compare Washington's approaches to dealing with Russia and Iran, notinginging that they were essentially the same.
“The economic choice between Iran and Russia is more or less, amplificating and replacing fuels,” he said, citing Russia as a “single-stunt pony” whose economy depends on fossil fuels.
These statements come at a time when the Trump administration's task is to increase exports of liquefied natural gas to Europe, replacing Russia, which is a much cheaper option for European consumers.
To do this, officials from the Trump administration, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to reject an “inappropriate” Nord Stream 2 pipeline project, which Trump said turned Berlin into a “prisoner” for Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly emphasized that an 11-billion-dollar Nord Stream 2 pipeline that doubles the capacity of an existing pipeline to 110 billion cubic meters is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington's fierce opposition to the project is simply motivated by economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
“I believe that we share the position that energy cannot be a means of exerting pressure and that consumers should have the opportunity to choose suppliers,” Russian Energy Minister Aleksandr Novak said at a meeting with U.S. Energy Secretary Rick Perry in Moscow in September.
The U.S. stance has backfired from Germany, which has reaffirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany's leading industrial organization, the German Industry Association (BDI), called on the U.S. to stay away from EU energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
“It’s a big problem for me when a third country interferes with our energy supply,” said Dieter Kempf, president of the German Association of Industries (BIDI), after a recent meeting between German chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warer is seriously considering running for president in 2020, the Massachusetts Senator says
Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts said on Saturday that she was seriously considering running for president in the interim elections.
At Holyoke City Hall in Massachusetts, Warren confirmed his candidacy consideration.
“It’s time for women to go to Washington and repair our broken government, and that includes the first lady,” he said.
“After November 6, I will seriously consider running for president.”
Warren also evaluated President Donald Trump’s actions at the City Hall, saying he “leading the country in the wrong direction.
"I'm really worried about what Donald Trump is doing with our democracy," he said.
Warren has criticized Trump and its Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh.
Warren tweeted on Friday, “Of course we need an FBI investigation before we vote.”
However, a poll published on Thursday showed that most of Warren's own voters don't think he's going to run in 2020.
According to a Poll by the Suffolk University's Political Investigation Center/Boston Globe, 58% of "probable" Massachusetts voters didn't think the senator should run.
32% supported such a candidate.
The poll showed more support for former governor Deval Patrick’s candidacy, 38% supported possible candidacy and 48% opposed it.
Other high-profile Democratic names discussed in relation to a possible 2020 nominee include former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.
Biden said he would formally decide by January, the Associated Press reported.
