18 states and the District of Columbia are challenging a new asylum policy
Eighteen countries and the District of Columbia support a legal challenge to a new U.S. policy that forbids refuge for victims fleeing gang or domestic violence.
Representatives from the 18 countries and regions filed a short statement from their students in Washington on Friday to support a challenging policy related to the asylum seeker, NBC News reported.
The applicant's full name, Grace v. Sessions smoked by the American Civic Freedoms Union in August, has not been published.
She said his accomplice “and his violent gang-member sons” abused him, but U.S. officials didn’t take his application for asylum on July 20.
He was detained in Texas.
Grace's supporting national lawyers described El Salvador, Hondura and Guatemala as producing a large number of U.S. asylum seekers, including gangs and domestic violence.
The new U.S. asylum policy reversed the 2014 decision of the Immigrant Appeals Board, which allowed undocumented immigrants to escape domestic violence in order to file for asylum.
Karl Racine, the District's Attorney General for Columbia, said in a statement on Friday that the new policy would “ignorate state, federal, and international law for decades.”
“The Federal Law requires that all asylum applications be resolved in accordance with certain facts and circumstances of the application, and such a requirement violates this principle,” the statement said.
Lawyers also argued in a brief statement that banning immigrants from entering would harm the U.S. economy, saying they would likely become entrepreneurs and “provide the necessary workforce.”
Attorney General Jeff Sessions ordered immigration magistrates not to refuge victims fleeing domestic abuse and gang violence in June.
“A request for asylum is available to those who leave their home country because of persecution or fear regarding race, religion, ethnicity, or a particular social group or political opinion,” Sessions said in its June 11 announcement.
