“The situation is very tense,” said 35-year-old mother Risa Kusuma, who comforted her febrile baby-shattered Palu city evacuation center.
“Every minute new people arrive by ambulance.
There's not enough clean water."
Residents returned to their destroyed homes, sorting out soaked things and trying to save everything they found.
Hundreds of people were injured and hospitals damaged by a 7.5-magnitude earthquake were overwhelmed.
Some of the injured Dwi Haris, suffering from a broken back and upper arm, were resting outside the Palu Army Hospital, where patients continued to be treated outdoors for strong aftershocks.
Tears filled his eyes when he recalled how a strong earthquake shook his fifth-floor hotel room, which he shared with his wife and daughter.
“There wasn’t time to save yourself.
I think I was pressed against the wall in ruins,” Haris told the Associated Press, adding that his family had come to the wedding.
“I heard my wife scream for help, but then there was silence.
I don't know what happened to him and my baby.
“I hope they’re not in danger.”
The U.S. ambassador accuses China of “bullyinging with racing ads”
A week after China's official newspaper published a four-page ad under U.S. daily news regarding the mutual daily benefits of U.S.-Chinese trade, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the American press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump cited an addition paid by China Daily last Wednesday at the Des Moines Register, the state's highest-circuited newspaper in Iowa, after China was accused of meddlinging in the November 6 U.S. congressional elections, which China denies.
Trump's indictment that Peking was trying to interfere in the U.S. election marked what U.S. officials described to Reuters as a new phase in Washington's expanding campaign to put pressure on China.
While it’s customary for foreign governments to do advertising to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently in a growing trade war where each other’s import tariffs are adjusted.
China's countermeasures at the start of the tariff war were designed to apprehend states such as Iowa's exporters because they supported the Trump Republican party, Chinese and U.S. experts have explained.
The U.S. ambassador to China and the former governor of Iowa, as well as Terry Branstad, China's largest exporter of agricultural products, said that Beijing harmed American workers, farmers and companies.
Branstad wrote in Sunday’s Des Moines Register opinion article: “China has now doubled its bullying by publishing agitation-advertisements in our free press.”
“In spreading its propaganda, the Chinese government uses America’s precious freedom of speech and the practice of a free press by placing its paid advertising on the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
“On the other hand, there are only limited voices at the street kiosk in Beijing and do not see the truly different opinions that the Chinese people may have about China’s alarming economic direction, given that the media is firmly at the hands of the Chinese Communist Party,” he added.
He added that one of China's most prominent newspapers declined to publish his article, but he did not say what kind of newspaper it was.
Analysts warn Republicans alienate female voters with Kavanaugh fiasco before the interim elections
While many Republicans are awaiting and defending Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh, who has been accused of multiple sexual assaults, analysts have warned that they will face a setback during the upcoming interim elections, especially from women.
Feelings surrounding the situation have been in the ceiling, and most Republicans have already shown that they want to move forward with voting.
These things cannot be undone,” Grant Reeher, Professor of Political Science at Maxwelle School at Syracuse University, said in an article published on Saturday for The Hill.
Reeher expressed doubts that Senator Jeff Flake's (R-Arizona) last-minute effort in connection with the FBI's investigation is enough to reassure angry voters.
“Women won’t forget what happened yesterday – they won’t forget it tomorrow or November,” said Karine Jean-Pierre, a senior adviser to the Progressive Group MoveOn and a spokeswoman for the Washington D.C.
“November is Coming!” on Friday morning reiterated a demonstration in their Senate corridor after Republicans, who control the Judiciary Committee, decided to proceed with Kavanaugh’s candidacy, despite testimony from Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
“Democratic enthusiasm and indulgence are starting to stay out of schedule,” unofficial political analyst Stu Rothenberg said.
“People say it’s already high, that’s true.
However, this may be higher, especially among suburban female voters and younger voters between the ages of 18 and 29, who don’t like the president and often miss the vote.”
Even before Ford's public testimony describing his allegations of a sexual assault against a Supreme Court nominee, analysts predicted a setback as Republicans moved forward with confirmation.
“It’s become very confusing for the GOP,” Michael Steele, former chairman of the Republican National Committee, told NBC News.
“It’s not just about the commission’s vote, the final vote, or whether Kavanaugh will be appointed, it’s also about how Republicans have handled it and how they’ve treated him,” Guy Cecil, head of Priorities U.S. (a group to help elect Democrats), drew attention.
However, the Americans seem to be a little divided, who, after testimonies, believe in Ford and who Kavanaugh, but a little more supporting the former.
YouGov's new poll found that 41% of respondents certainly or probably believed in Ford's testimony, while 35% said they definitely or probably believed in Kavanaugh.
In addition, 38% said they thought Kavanaugh had apparently or certainly lied at the time of his testimony, while only 30% thought the same about Ford.
Thanks to Flake, the FBI is currently investigating Ford's allegations and at least one other allegation against Deborah Ramirez, The Guardian reported.
Ford testified under oath in front of the Senate Judiciary Committee last week that Kavanaugh attacked him when he was 17.
Ramirez argues that the Supreme Court candidate showed him her genitals when they attended a party while studying at Yale in the 1980s.
World Wide Web's inventor plans to launch a new internet that will confront Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, has launched a start-up trying to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
Inrupt, the latest project of the technology legender, is a company that will build the Berners-Lee open-source Solid platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people have been given access to information.
In an exceptional questioning to the Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that Inrupt’s goal is “ruling the world.”
“We have to do it now,” he said about the start-up.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid technology to allow people to create their own “personal online database” or POD.
It can contain contact lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library, and other personal and competent tools.
It's like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify, which are all available in one browser and at the same time.
What makes this personal online database unique is that the user has the opportunity to choose who can access this information.
The company calls it “personal authority through data.”
According to company CEO John Bruce, Inrupt's idea that the company would bring sources, the process, and relevant skills to help make Solid available to everyone.
At the moment, the company consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, an IBM-bought security platform, some developers who have been hired to work on a project under contract, and a community of volunteer encoders.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world can create their decentralized applications using the tools on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team are not talking to “Facebook and Google, whether to introduce a complete change if all their business models are fully supported overnight.
“We don’t ask their permission.”
In a post published in Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote that Inrupt's goal is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem to help protect the integrity and quality of the new web created on the basis of Solid.”
In 1994, Berners-Lee changed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has had an influential vote in the debate on the impartiality of the network.
Even with the launch of Inrupt, Berners-Lee remains the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I am extremely optimistic about the next era of the web,” Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: A memorial stone of a clergyman who received the Victoria Cross in World War
The only Church of England to receive the Victoria Crucifix during the First World War celebrated the 100th Anniversary in its hometown.
Lieutenant Colonel Reverend Bernard Vann fought his badge of honour on September 29, 1918, in an attack by Bellenglis and Lehaucourt.
However, he was killed four days later through a sniper's bullet and did not know that he had received the highest British army decoration.
His two grandsons opened a memorial stone on Saturday in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of his grandsons, Michael Vann, said it would be “ingeniously symbolic” if the stone was opened exactly 100 years after his grandfather’s award-winning fight.
According to London Gazette, on 29 September 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Vann commanded his battalion over Canal de Saint-Quentin “through a very thick fog and gun and machine gun hail.”
Later, he rushed to the offensive line and brought it forward with “the greatest galance” before rushing alone to a machine gun and knocking out three opponents.
Lieutenant Colonel Vanni was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918, when there was only a little more than a month left until the end of the war.
Michael Vann (72) said his grandfather’s action was “something I know I’ll never be able to repeat, but for which I will always be grateful.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, laid a wreath after a parade led by Brentwood’s conking youth band.
Michael Vann said he was “a great honor to participate in this parade” and added that “the presence and support of so many people shows that a true hero can be appreciated.”
M.M. fans were up all night to watch Bellator 206, but only saw Peppa in the Piglet
Imagine you've been up all night to watch Bellator 206, but that's not gonna be shown to you.
San Jose's match included 13 fights, including six on the main card, and was streamed live on Channel 5 in the United Kingdom throughout the night.
At 6 a.m., when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to confront each other, UK viewers lost their mounts as a Piggy Peppa appeared on the screen.
Many weren't impressed with it, especially those who had stayed awake until the early hours to see precisely this struggle.
One fan on Twitter described the transition to children’s comics as “some kind of sick joke.”
“It’s a government regulation that at 6:00 a.m. the content was no longer appropriate and they had to switch to a children’s program,” explained Dave Schwartz, Bellator’s senior vice president for marketing and communications when asked about the transfer.
“Peppa the Pig”, yes.”
Bellator's president, Scott Coker, said they planned to work on drawing up their own schedule in order to take greater account of UK viewers in the future.
“If I think about playback now, I think we will be able to solve this problem in the future,” Coker said.
“But if it’s 6 o’clock on a Sunday morning, we can’t do it until our own Sunday, which is their Monday.
But we're working on it.
Believe me when the program was changed, there was a lot of back and forth text, and not all of them were friendly.
We tried to fix it, we thought it was a technical malfunction.
But it wasn't, it was a government rule.
I can promise it won't happen next time.
We're going to show five - as we usually do instead of six fights - we tried to offer more to the fans, and we just switched with time.
It’s a bad situation.”
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt “inferiority” about sexuality
Olympic diver Tom Daley says he grew up feeling he's inferiority because of his sexuality - but it gave him the motivation to succeed.
A 24-year-old young man said he didn't realize until he went to high school that "not everyone is like him."
Speaking on Lauren Laverne's Radio 4 show Desert Island Discs, she said she's talking about gay rights to give hope to others.
Becoming a parent also changed her attitude towards winning the Olympics, giving the feeling that it is no longer so important.
Kirsty Young, the show's usual long-time presenter, had to take a few months off because of her illness.
In Laverne's first show, Daley said she felt less valuable as she grew up than others because it wasn't socially acceptable if you liked boys and girls.
“Until to this day, those feelings that I’m not worthy and I feel different have given me power and strength to be successful.”
He wanted to prove that he deserved “something” and didn’t disappoint others when they finally found out about his sexuality.
A two-time Bronze Olympian champion has become the leader of a highly valued LGBT campaign, using his appearance at this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to appeal to more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he's talking about it because he's lucky he'd be able to live open without consequences and want to give hope to others.
The three-time world champion said that falling in love with the man, American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013 – “hit him as a surprise.”
Daley married a 20-year-old Oscar winner last year, but she said the age difference has never been a problem.
“When you’re young, you go through so much” – he went to his first Olympics at the age of 14, and his father died of cancer three years later—then it was difficult to find someone of the same age who had experienced similar ups and downs.
The couple became parents in June, they have a son named Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley said his “whole prospect” has changed.
“If you’d asked me last year, it was all about winning a gold medal,” he said.
"But you know, there's things that are more important than Olympic gold medals.
My Olympic gold medal is Robbie.”
His son was named after his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40, following the diagnosis of a brain tumor.
Daley said his father didn't agree to die, and one of the last things he asked for was whether they had tickets to London in 2012 because he wanted to sit in the front row.
“I couldn’t tell him, ‘Dad, you can’t sit in the front row, because by that time you won’t be,” he said.
“I held his hand when he stopped breathing, and it wasn’t until after he had actually stopped breathing and was dead, I finally admitted that he wasn’t invincible,” he said.
Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics the following year and won bronze.
“I just knew it was what I’d been dreaming about all my life – diving in front of a home crowd at the Olympics, there’s no better feeling about it,” he said.
It also inspired his first song selection - Heather Small’s Proud – which helped him in preparation for the Olympics and still evokes goose bumps on him.
Desert Island Discs will air BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:115 BST.
Mickelson was stripped from the Ryder Cup on Saturday
Phil Mickelson, an American, sets a record on Sunday when he plays his 47th Ryder Cup game, but he has to improve his form in order to avoid an unfortunate milestone.
Mickelson, who will play a record 12th time every two-year event, was stripped from the match by Captain Jim Furyk for Saturday's doubles and a four-ball match.
Instead of being at the center of the action, as it has often been for the U.S., a five-time winner split his day into two, being a cheerleader and working on his game in hopes of improving it.
Never the most direct striker, not even at the peak of his career, a 48-year-old is not particularly suitable for playing on a narrow Le Golf National golf course, where a long rough track constantly punishes misguided strokes.
And if the field itself isn't terrifying enough, Mickelson will be confronted on Sunday, his ninth race, with the precise British Open champion Francesco Molinar, who has joined forces with beginner Tommy Fleetwood to win all of his four races this week.
If the Americans, who are four points behind at the start of the 12 single games, start fiercely, Mickelson's competition can be extremely important.
Furyk expressed confidence in her husband, but he didn't have much else to add.
“He fully understood his part today, patted me on the shoulder, put his hand around my shoulder and said he was ready for tomorrow,” Furyk added.
"He's very confident.
He's a celebrity and he's given as much to our teams both in the past and this week.
I probably didn't see him playing two games.
I saw more, but that's how it went, and we had to choose such a path.
He wants to be outside, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson overtakes Nick Faldo's record on Sunday as the player with the most Ryder Cup games.
It can mark the end of his Ryder Cup career, which, however, has never matched his personal record.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 losses and seven draws, but Furyk said his presence has made an extra mental contribution to the team.
“He’s funny, mocking, funny, he likes to have fun with people and he’s just great in the team room,” he explained.
“I think the younger players had a lot of fun going against him this week and it was nice to see it.
He offers much more than just a game.”
The captain of Europe, Thomas Bjorn, knows that a great lead could soon disappear
Thomas Bjorn, the captain of Europe, knows from his own experience that the great leadership of the Ryder Cup's last day at the beginning of individual games can easily turn into an unpleasant experience.
The Dane made public in the 1997 Valderrama competition, where a five-point lead was held in front of the Americans together with Captain Seve Ballesteros, but it had drastically diminished when it reached the finish line, with only 141⁄2 131⁄2.
“You even remember that we had a big lead in Valderrama; we also had great success at Brookline, where we lost and in Valderrama, where we won, this victory came very narrowly,” said Bjorn in the picture after the Class of 2018 victory 5 : 3 on Friday and with yesterday’s success 10 : 6 at Le Golf National.
So history shows me and the whole team that this could happen again.
You're doing your best tomorrow.
You're going out and doing the right things.
It's not over until you have any points.
We have a goal, which is to win the trophy, so let's focus on it.
I’ve always said, I’m focusing on the 12 players on our side, but we’re very aware of who our opponents are – the best players in the world.”
Dissatisfied with how his players have played on this difficult golf course, Bjorn added: “I’m not going to rush things up.
Tomorrow it will be completely new obstacles.
There'll be personal performances tomorrow, so it'll be different.
It's nice to be with an opponent when things go well, but if you're there personally, you'll be fully tested as a golfer.
This is a message that you have to convey to the players - do your best tomorrow.
Now that you’re leaving your partner behind, they’re gonna have to get and do it on their own.”
Unlike Bjorn, Jim Furyk expects his players to perform better personally than they do with opponents, except for Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who scored three out of four points.
Furyk himself has experienced both over the last few days, being part of Brookline’s winning team, before becoming a loss when Europe defeated “Medinah the Miracle.”
“Remembrance of every goddamn word,” he said in response, when asked how in 1999 Captain Ben Crenshaw encouraged his players to put everything into their game on the last day.
“We will have 12 important games tomorrow, but you have to start fiercely, as in Brookline and like in Medinah.
If the momentum goes in one direction, it puts a lot of pressure on average competitions.
Let’s set up our team accordingly and put the boys on the field in a way where we feel that a miracle can happen tomorrow.”
Thomas has been taskeded with trying to lead the competition and he will face Rory McIlroy, with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter as the best Europeans in the competition.
“I put my boys in such a queue, because I think that’s how we’re properly covered,” Bjorn said of his selection of singles.
Germany's new warship postponed again
The German navy's newest high sailboat should have been deployed in 2014, replacing obsolete Cold-War warships, but that won't happen until next year due to flawed systems and snowballon-grown costs, local media reported.
The introduction of the new Baden-Wuerttemberg high-speed sailboat control ship “Rheinland-Palatinate” has now been postponed to the first half of 2019, according to a military press release published in the Die Zeit newspaper.
The ship should have joined the navy as early as 2014, but the worrying post-handover problems did not make the fate of this ambitious project easy.
Four Baden-Wuerttemberg ships, commissioned by the navy in 2007, will be replaced by outdated Bremen-class high-speed sailors.
It's understandable that they have a powerful cannon, a number of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, and some concealment technologies, such as reducing radars, infrared and audible signals.
Other important options include longer maintenance periods - it should be possible to send the latest high-speed sailboats out of the home port for up to two years.
Continuous delays, however, mean that state-of-the-art warships, which promise to strengthen power abroad in Germany, will become obsolete by the times of service.
The failed F125 high sailboat appeared on the news last year when the German navy officially refused to deploy the ship and returned it to Hamburg's Blohm & Voss shipyard.
It was the first time the Navy has returned the ship to the shipbuilder after receiving it.
Little was known about the reasons for the return, but German media cited a number of important “software and hardware deficiencies” that made the warship unusable if they had been used in a combat mission.
Software shortages were particularly significant, as Baden-Württemberg class ships are operated by a crew of about 120 sailors—just half of an older Bremen-class quick sailboat workforce.
It also emerged that the ship is remarkably overweight, which reduces its performance and limits the navy's ability to add future innovations.
“Rheinland-Palatinate” weighing 7000 tons is believed to be twice as heavier as ships of the same class, used by the Germans in World War II.
In addition to faulty hardware, the price of the entire project, including team training, also becomes a problem.
It has been said that this amounts to 3.1 billion euros (3.6 billion dollars) compared to the original 2.2 billion euros.
Problems related to newer high sailboats are becoming especially important in the light of recent warnings that German naval power is shrinking.
At the beginning of the year, Hans-Peter Bartels, chairman of the defense committee of the German parliament, admitted that the navy had actually run out of “extinguished ships.”
The official said that this topic has grown as a snowball over time, because the old ships were removed, but there were no replacement ships.
He expressed regret that none of the Baden-Wuerttembrg-class high-speed sailboats could join the navy.
National Trust explores the secret life of bats
A new study in the Scottish Highlands aims to reveal how bats use the landscape to search for their food.
It is hoped that the results will provide new light on the behavior of these unique flying mammals and help guide future protective measures.
A study by researchers at the National Trust in Scotland tracks conventional and soprany pipelles, as well as brown long-eared and Daubenton bats, in the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders are placed in the main locations of the area to monitor the activity of bats throughout the season.
NHS employees and volunteers also conduct field research using handheld sensors.
The competent assessment of all recordings determines the frequency of bat cries and the activity of the species.
A habitat map and a report will then be created to create a detailed landscape picture of their behavior.
NTS Conservation Advisor Rob Dewar hopes the results will show which habitats are the most important for bats and how different species use them.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management, such as the creation of meadows and how best to preserve forests for bats and other related species.
In Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom, bat populations have declined significantly in the last century.
They are threatened by construction and development, which affects their habitats and their loss.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a threat, as can flying papers and the chemical treatment of some building materials, as well as pet cat attacks.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, due to their nocturnal hunting, their ears are more useful in catching prey than their eyes.
They use complex echo-location techniques to identify insects and obstructions on their flight trajectory.
More than 270 historical buildings across the country, the NTS responsible for the maintenance of 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land, take bats very seriously.
They have ten trained experts who regularly carry out examinations, inspections, and sometimes save.
The organization has even created Scotland’s first and only special bat reserve in Threave in Dumfrie and Galloways, which is inhabited by eight of Scotland’s ten bat species.
Property manager David Thompson says this area is the perfect territory for them.
“At Threave, we have a large area for bats,” he said.
“We have old buildings, a lot of old trees and a big good habitat.
But there’s still a lot to discover about bats, so the work we’re doing here and in other areas will help us better understand what they need.”
He emphasizes the importance of controlling bats before embarking on maintenance activities on the property, as the unwanted destruction of a single place of gestation and childbirth can kill up to 400 females and young people, and even destroy the entire local population.
Bats are protected and it is illegal to kill, disturb or destroy them.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish officer of the Bat Defense League, has urged the public to contribute.
He said: “We still have so much to learn about our bats, and we just don’t know many of our species and how their populations feed.”
Ronaldo rejects rape allegations while lawyers plan to sue German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo has dismissed rape allegations about him as “fake news,” saying people “want to promote themselves” using his name.
His lawyers plan to sue the German newspaper Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The Portuguese and Juventus attackers have been accused of raping Kathryn Mayorga in a Las Vegas hotel room in 2009.
She has reportedly paid 330 643.10 euros for the woman not to talk about the incident, Der Spiegel said on Friday.
Addressing her 142 million followers in an Instagram live video hours after the allegations, Ronaldo (33) was announced to be “fake news.”
“No, no, no, no, no.
What has been said today is fake news,” a five-time Ballon d’Or winner told the news.
They want to promote themselves with my name.
It's normal.
They want to become famous with my name, but it's part of the job.
“I’m a lucky man and I’m all right,” the player added with a smile.
According to Reuters, Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel in connection with “an unauthorized suspicion of privacy.”
Christian Schertz, a lawyer, said the player is seeking compensation for “moral damage that corresponds to the severity of the violation, which is probably one of the most serious violations of personal rights in recent years.”
The alleged incident took place in June 2009 in a suite at Palms Hotel and Casino in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga reportedly went back to the player's hotel room, where he was allegedly raped based on documents filed with the Clark County District Court in Nevada.
Mayorga claims that Ronaldo fell to his knees after an alleged incident and told him that he was a “99 percent good man” of whom “one percent fail.”
The documents claim that Ronaldo confirmed the couple's sexual relationship, but this was not unanimous.
Mayorga also claims that he went to the police and took photos of the injuries in the hospital, but later agreed to an out-of-court settlement because he was afraid of “revenge” and was concerned about “public humiliation.”
The 34-year-old woman now says that she is now trying to refute the solution, as the alleged case continues to traumatize her.
Ronaldo had left Manchester United at the time of the alleged attack and joined Real Madrid, and this summer he moved to Italy in connection with a 100-million-euro deal by giant Juve.
Brexit: The UK could start to repentance of automakers leaving forever
The United Kingdom “would regret it forever” if it lost her position as CEO of the world’s automotive industry after Brexit, business secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that the Toyota UK had told the BBC that if Britain left the EU without a deal, it would temporarily stop their production at a factory in Burnaston near Derby.
“We need an agreement,” Clark said.
The Japanese automaker said that the impact of border crossing stoppages without a Brexit deal could cost jobs.
Burnaston's factory, which manufactures Toyota Auris and Avennis vehicles, produced nearly 150 000 cars last year, 90% of which was exported to the rest of the European Union.
“My opinion is that if the UK leaves the EU at the end of March, we will see a stop in the production process at its factory,” said Marvin Cooke, managing director of Toyota at Burnaston.
Other UK automakers have also feared leaving the EU without an agreement, worryinginging the functioning of cross-border trade, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
BMW, for example, says that it will close the Mini factory in Oxford for one month after Brexit.
The main concerns of car manufacturers relate to supply chain risks in the absence of a Brexit deal.
Toyota's production line works according to the needs, where spare parts arrive from both the United Kingdom and the EU every 37 minutes.
If the UK leaves the EU without an agreement signed on 29 March, there may be disturbances at the border, which according to the industry could lead to delays and shortages.
Toyota wouldn't be able to keep more than one-day supply at its Derbyshire plant, which is why production has to be stopped.
Clark said Theresa May Chequers’ plan regarding the EU’s future relationship was “exactly calibrated to avoid such checks at the border.”
“We need an agreement. We want the best solution that would allow for what we want and not only to take advantage of current success, but all the opportunity,” he told BBC Radio 4.
“Evidence not only from Toyota, but also from other manufacturers, shows that we need to be able to continue what has been a very successful set of supply chains.”
Toyota couldn't say how long production would be suspended, but in the long term warned that additional costs would reduce the yard's competitiveness and ultimately cost jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, a spokeswoman for the Unite union who has worked at Burnaston for 24 years, said his members are increasingly concerned: “From our own experience, we know that if jobs disappear, they won’t come back.
A government spokesperson said: “We have presented an accurate and reliable plan for our future relations with the EU.”
Trump's meeting with Rosenstein could be postponed again, the White House says
Donald Trump's high-staking meeting with Attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be postponed for another week as the fight against top-level nominee Brett Kavanaugh continues, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein is following the work of special commissioner Robert Mueller, who is investigating Russian election meddling, links between Trump's assistants and Russia, and possible obstruction of justice by the president.
Whether Trump is firing a deputy attorney-at-learning attorney general and thereby threatening Mueller's independence has been igniting Washington rumours for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times announced that Rosenstein had discussed the use of eavesdropping tools to record conversations with Trump and the possibility of removing the president through the 25th Amendment.
Rosenstein didn't confirm that.
But last Monday, he went to the White House, announcing that he was going to resign.
Instead, it was announced on Thursday at a meeting with Trump, who would then attend the U.N. in New York at that time.
Trump said he didn't want to fire Rosenstein, but then the meeting was postponeded to avoid concessitionate the Senate Judicial Commission, where Kavanaugh and one of the women who have accused him of sexual abuse, Dr. Christine Blasey Ford, both gave testimonies.
On Friday, Trump ordered a one-week FBI investigation into the allegations against Kavanaugh, postponing the Senate vote.
Trump's spokeswoman Sarah Sanders appeared on Fox News on Sunday.
When asked about the meeting with Rosenstein: “This date has not yet been set, it may be this week, but it may be postponed for another week, taking into account all the things that are ongoing in relation to the Supreme Court.
But let’s look at it and keep the press up to date, as always.”
Some journalists would dispute this claim: Sanders hasn't held a White House press conference since September 10.
Chris Wallace asked why.
Sanders said the lack of briefings wasn’t due to deliberately disappointinging TV journalists, although he added: “I don’t dispute the fact that they’ve been disappointed.”
He then made direct contact between Trump and the press.
The president is doing more Q&A sessions than any president before him," he said without adding evidence: “We’ve looked at these numbers.”
Briefings will continue, Sanders confirmed, but “if the press has the opportunity to ask the president of the United States for questions directly, it’s much better than talking to me.
We're trying a lot to do, and you've seen how we've done this over the last couple of weeks, so it'll take place during a press conference if you can talk to the President of the United States."
Trump regularly answers questions when leaving the White House or by attending open sessions or press conferences with guests.
Solo's press conferences are rare.
This week in New York, the president perhaps demonstrated why, having gathered in front of journalists in a freewheeling and sometimes bizarre appearance.
Health minister writes NHS for EU workers in Scotland over Brexit fears
The Minister of Health has written to EU employees working in the NHS in Scotland to express the country's gratitude and wish their post-Brexitary presence.
Jeane Freeman MSP sent a letter with less than six months left until the UK's withdrawal from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already committed itself to covering the costs associated with established status for EU citizens working in their public services.
Freeman wrote: “Negotiations on residence between the UK and the EU have resumed in the summer, heading towards the expected decisions this autumn.
However, the UK government has also made preparations for a possible non-agreement scenario.
I know it's a very disturbing time for you.
That's why I now wanted to replicate how much I appreciate each employee's contribution, regardless of their nationality.
Professionals from across the EU and elsewhere bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of health services and benefit patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is your home and we want you to stay.”
Christion Abercrombie has undergone an emergency surgery after a head injury
Tennessee State Tigers support player Christion Abercrombie underwent an emergency surgery following Saturday's head injury and defeat 31 : 27 from Vanderb's Commodores, according to Tennessee's Mike Organ.
Tennessee State manager Rod Reed told reporters that the injury happened just before half-time.
“He fell on his side and just collapsed,” Reed said.
Trainers and medical staff gave Abercrombie oxygen on their sides before being placed on a stretcher was removed from the field for further inspection.
Tennessee State official told Chris Harris (WSMV - Nashville, Tennessee) that Abercrombie's surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center has ended.
Harris added that there are no further details about the type/scope of the injunction, and Tennessee State is trying to find out when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a second-year student with a red shirt, spent his first season at Tennessee State since his transfer from Illinois.
He had five fights on Saturday before leaving the game, making a total of 18 matches this season.
Foreign buyers have to pay a higher stamp duty when buying real estate in the United Kingdom
Foreign buyers will have to pay a higher stamp duty if they buy real estate in the United Kingdom, using this extra money to help the homeless under new Tory plans
This move will disarm Corbyn's aspiration to attract young voters
Stamping tax is levied on those who do not pay tax in the United Kingdom
The Treasury Department hopes it will create up to 120 million pounds a year to help the homeless
Foreign buyers will have to pay a higher stamp tax rate if they buy real estate in the UK using this extra money to help the homeless, Theresa May said today.
The movement is seen as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn's efforts to attract young voters with promises to provide them with affordable housing and target high-income people.
Stem tax is levied on individuals and companies that do not pay tax in the UK, directing extra money towards the government's desire to combat homelessness.
The premium – which is in addition to the current stamp duty, including higher levels that were imposed two years ago for the second home and for on-lease purchases – can be up to three percent.
The Ministry of Finance hopes that this movement will generate up to 120 million pounds a year.
It is estimated that 13% of the properties purchased in London belong to non-UK residents, which raises prices and makes it difficult for first-time buyers to get their feet when purchasing housing.
Many of the country’s prosperous regions — especially in the capital — have become “ghost cities” thanks to a large number of foreign buyers who spend most of their time outside the country.
A new policy arrives just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for a reduction in stamp duty to help young people acquire their first home.
He accused large construction companies of keeping real estate prices high when they were buying land but not using it, and urged May to give up quotas on affordable homes to correct Britain’s “housing shame.”
Corbyn has announced an eye-catching housing reform, including rent control and ending the harassment of innocent people.
He also wants to give the councils greater powers to build new homes.
May said: “Last year I pledged that as Prime Minister I would commit myself to the restoration of the British dream – that life would be better for every new generation.
And that means repairing our broken housing market.
Britain is always open to people who want to live, work and build a life here.
At the same time, it is right that individuals who do not live in the UK, as well as companies abroad, find it as easy to buy homes as hard-working British residents.
For too many people, the dream of owning a home has become too distant, and harsh sleeping conditions are a normal reality.”
Jack Ross: “My Ultimate Goal is Managing Scotland”
Sunderlan’s leader, Jack Ross, says his “final goal” is to become Scotland’s leader at some point.
The Scot (42) is delighted with the challenge of reviving the North-East club, who are currently in third place in League One with only 3 points behind.
He moved to Stadium of Light this summer after taking St Mirren back to the Scottish Champions League last season.
“I wanted to represent my country as a player.
I got to class B and that’s the end of it,” Ross told Sportsound.
“But I grew up with my dad looking at Hampden, Scotland, and that’s always something that has pulled me back.
However, this opportunity will only come if I am successful in managing the club.”
Rosse's predecessors as leader of Sunderland have been Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
The former director of Alloa Athletic says he wasn't afraid of such big and well-known names, having previously resisted both Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
“For me, it’s a success right now if I can take this club to the Champions League.”
Due to its structure and facilities, this club undoubtedly belongs to the Champions League,” he said.
“It’s not an easy task to get there, but I only see myself successful if I manage to take the club back there.”
Ross has only worked as a manager for three years, serving as Dumbarton's deputy director and for 15 months among Hearts executive staff.
He then helped Alloa recover from falling to third level and making St Mirren's lowest position as the winner of the next championship.
And Ross says he's feeling more comfortable now that he's been in Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren, and Hamilton Academical during his career.
“It was a real crossroads,” he recalled the takeover of Alloa.
“I really believed that leadership is better for me than playing.
It feels strange, because I managed, got a reasonable income and enjoyed a few highlights.
Playing can be difficult.
There are many things that need to be done on a weekly basis.
I'm still experiencing this with the tension and pressure of my work in mind, but management just feels right.
I’ve always wanted to lead, and now I’m doing it. I feel more comfortable now than I’ve ever been in adulthood
You can listen to a full interview on Sunday's September 30 episode of Sportsound, at Radio Scotland from 12:00 to 13:00 BST
According to the study, the best time for a pint is Saturday at 17.30
The summer heat has improved the turnover of British troubled pubs, but more pressure has arisen on restaurant chains.
The figures showed that the turnover of pubs and bars rose by 2.7% in July, but that of restaurants fell by 4.8%.
These figures were compiled by Peter Martin, spokesman for business consulting company CGA: “Continuing sunshine and England’s longer-than-expected participation in the World Championships meant that July was similar to June, when pub turnover increased by 2.8 per cent, except that it was even more difficult for restaurants.
In June, the 1.8% decrease in restaurant trading deteriorated further in July.
Beverage-based pubs and bars traded the most successfully, and the restaurants trying to like are the worst.
Food-based pubs also suffered regardless of the sun, although not as dramatically as restauratees.
It seems that people just wanted to go out for a drink.
Between managed pubs and bars, beverage sales increased by 6.6 percent during the month, while food sales fell three percent.”
Paul Newman, a leisure and hospitality analyst, said: “These results will continue the trend we’ve been seeing since late April.
Weather and the influence of major social or sporting events remain the biggest factor when it comes to sales outside the home market.
Unsurprisingly, restaurant chains continue to fight, although a drop in sales of 4.8 percent compared to the previous year is especially painful in addition to the ongoing price pressure.
A long and hot summer could not have come at a worse time for food companies, and only time will tell whether the more moderated temperatures we have experienced in August will provide some essential relief.”
Growth in total sales of pubs and restaurants, including new openings, was 2.7 per cent in July, reflecting a slowdown in brand-related institutions.
Coffer Peach Tracker's sales tracker in the UK pubs, bars and restaurants sector collects and analyzes performance data from 47 action groups with a total turnover of more than 9 billion pounds, and this serves as an established industry benchmark.
