However, this should be started.
Talk to your husband and children: You can not prepare your family for care if they do not know your wishes in advance.
Cooperate with your advisor and family by discussinging where and how to receive care, because these options can be extremely important in terms of costs.
Consult with your financial advisor: Your advisor can have a good plan on how to pay for these expenses.
Your financing options for long-term care may include, for example, a traditional long-term care insurance policy, a hybrid life insurance policy of financial value to help cover those costs, or proprietary self-insurance - as long as you have the money you need.
Prepare your legal documents: Stop all possible legal battles already in the future.
Worry for yourself a power of attorney for health services to appoint a trustee to monitor your medical care and to ensure that professionals comply with your wishes while you are unable to communicate.
Also, consider a trustee in relation to your financiary.
Choose a reliable person to make financial decisions for you and ensure that your invoices are paid even if you are incapacitated.
