Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed as a result of a powerful earthquake and tsunami that hit the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, officials said, adding that the death toll is still rising.
As communications have been severed, rescuers have not received any information from Palu's northern Donggala regency, which is closer to the epicenter of the 7.5-magnitude earthquake.
More than 16 000 people were evacuated after the disaster.
Here are some important facts about Palu and Donggala on Sulawesi Island:
Palu is the provincial capital of Central Sulawesi, located on the west coast of the island of Sulawesi, and had an estimated 379,800 inhabitants in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when an earthquake and tsunami hit it unexpectedly.
Donggala regenty is an area that extends more than 300 km (180 miles) in the northwest of Sulawesi Island.
Regency, which is an administrative area under the provincial authority, had an estimated 299 200 inhabitants in 2017.
Fishing and agriculture are the main economic activities in the Central Sulawesi province, in particular the Donggala coastal area.
Nickel mining is also important in the province, which is mainly concentrated on the opposite coast of Morowal, Sulawesi.
According to the Indonesia Disaster Mitigation Agency, tsunamis have hit Palu and Donggala several times in the last 100 years.
In 1938 the tsunami in Donggala killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of houses.
In 1996, the tsunami struck the western part of Donggala, killing nine.
Indonesia is seismically stationed in the Pacific Circle of Fire and is regularly hit by earthquakes.
Here are some of the biggest earthquakes and tsunamis of recent years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of Aceh Province of Indonesia in northern Sumatra unleashed a tsunami that hits 14 countries and killed 226 000 people along the Indian Ocean coast, more than half of them in Aceh.
2005: A series of strong earthquakes hit Sumatra's west coast in March and the beginning of April.
Hundreds died on the island of Nias on the Sumatra coast.
2006: The 6.8-degree earthquake struck Indonesia's most populated island of Java from the south, creating a tsunami that hit the southern coast and killed nearly 700 people.
2009: A 7.6-degree earthquake struck near Padang, the capital of West Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-degree earthquake hit one of Sumatra Islands - the island of Mentawai, unleashing a tsunami up to a height of up to 10 meters, which destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A deep quarry hit Pidie Jaya's regency in Aceh, sowing destruction and panicking, reminiscing people of the deadly earthquake of 2004 and tsunami-brought destruction.
There was no tsunami at that time, but more than 100 people were killed in the collapsed buildings.
2018: Major earthquakes hit Lombok tourist islands in Indonesia, killing more than 500 people, mainly in the northern side of the island.
An earthquake destroyed thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists temporarily distressed.
Sarah Palin's eldest son has been arrested on charges of domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska governor and vice presidential nominee Sarah Palin, has been arrested in connection with the attack.
According to a report published by the Alaska State Troopers on Saturday, Palin (29) was arrested in Wasilla, Alaska, regarding suspicions of domestic violence, interferencing in a report related to domestic violence, and resistancing arrest.
