In NYC, federal prosecutors are seeking a rare death penalty for a suspect in a terrorist attack
Federal prosecutors in New York apply for the death penalty on Sayfullo Saipov, who is suspected of carrying out a terrorist attack in New York that killed eight people. This rare sentence has not been carried out for federal crime since 1953.
Saipov (30) allegedly used Home Depot’s rental car to attack the West Side highway on the Lower Manhattan, mowing both pedestrians and cyclists on his way in October.
According to the death penalty issued in the southern district of New York, prosecutors are required to prove that Saipov deliberately killed eight of the victims and also caused serious bodily injuries.
In both cases, on the basis of judicial documents, it is a possible death penalty.
A few weeks after the attack, a federal junior body charged Saipov with a 22-point indictment containing eight murder charges with extortion, generally used by federal prosecutors for organized criminal offences, and indictment of violence and the destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required “intensive planning and premeditation,” prosecutors said, describing Saipov’s actions as “dreadful, cruel and depraved.”
“Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injuries, damage and losses to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelin, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruch, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco.
Five victims were Argentine tourists.
It's been ten years since New York City's Southern District Cournary's last death trial.
Khalid Barnes was convicted of the murder of two drug traffickers, but was ultimately sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The death penalty was last carried out in 1953 by Julius and Ethel Rosenberg in a federal case in New York City, when a married couple was executed after being sentenced to conspiracy to carry out espionage for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years ago.
Both Rosenbergs were executed in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, an indigenous Uzbeck, showed a lack of remorse in the days and months following the attack, according to court documents.
Police said he had told investigators that he was pleased with what had been done.
According to the indictment, Saipov told the authorities that he was inspired to commit the attack by ISIS videos viewed on his phone.
According to the police, he also wanted the ISIS flag to be displayed in his hospital room.
He has not pleaded guilty in a 22-point indictment.
David Patton, one of Saipov’s public defenders, said they were “obviously disappointed” in the procedural decision.
“In our opinion, the decision to apply for the death penalty extends the trauma of everyone involved instead of a lifetime prison sentence without the possibility of release,” Patton said.
Saipov's defense team previously asked prosecutors not to apply for the death penalty.
