New information on water flowering: decreased concentration in Pinellas, Manathesis and Sarasota
The most recent report from the Florida Committee on Fisheries and Wildlife (FWC) shows a reduction in the overall concentration of blooming algae in the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, there have been reports of more patchy prosperity in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties, suggesting decreasing concentrations.
The water flower range is about 130 miles (209 km) along the coast from the northern part of Pinellas County to the southern part of Lee County.
Spots can be found 10 miles (16 km) away from Hillsborough County coast, but there have been fewer spots compared to last week.
Water flowering has also been observed in Pasco County.
Last week there have been reports in Pinellas County the Average Concentration on the Coast or Offshore, in Hillsborough Counties from low to high concentrations on the open sea, in Manatee County from background to high concentrations, in Sarasota County on the coast and off-sea background to high-concent, in Charlotte County from background to medium concentration, in Lee County on the coast and offshore from background to high concentration
Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties will continue to report respiratory irritation.
There had been no reports of respiratory irritation in northwestern Florida over the past week.
